# ClangSharpPInvokeGenerator response file for everything macro-shaped, plus the handful of libc
# declarations this library needs.
#
# Separate from pipewire.rsp for one reason, and it is not the one the old drm.rsp gave. Emitting
# macros needs --generate macro-bindings, and that switch makes the run emit around 215 diagnostics
# from CompoundLiteralExpr macros (the SPA_POD_INIT_* family) and from function-like macros, which
# it skips. Those are inherent and harmless. The ABI pass fails on *any* diagnostic, and that
# strictness is what catches a type landing in the wrong namespace or an anonymous enum decaying
# into loose constants - so the noisy work is done here instead, where the guard can be narrow and
# explicit about which classes it tolerates.
#
# (The old comment claiming macro-bindings is *fatal* on the PipeWire headers was wrong. It exits 0.
# The reason to split is the diagnostic noise, not a failure.)
#
# --file is deliberately absent: generate.sh supplies the composite root. Naming it here as well
# makes it a second root and every declaration is emitted twice - 309 constants became 618 that way.

# - PipeWire's property keys, interface type names and interface versions -
#
# keys.h was hand-maintained as 59 of 192 constants until the list was audited; the absences
# included node.latency (the only way to ask for a quantum), node.group (how two nodes are forced
# onto one scheduling cycle) and stream.capture.sink (how a sink's monitor is captured).
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/keys.h

# SPA has its own key namespace, and some of what this library reads lives there rather than in
# PipeWire's keys.h - audio.position among them.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/utils/keys.h
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/monitor/device.h
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/param/audio/raw.h
# SPA_NODE_FLAG_*, SPA_NODE_CHANGE_MASK_*, SPA_PORT_FLAG_*, SPA_PORT_CHANGE_MASK_*: the bits an
# exported node announces itself with. Copied by hand they were wrong twice over (RT announced as
# OUT_PORT_CONFIG), and a wrong bit is silently accepted by the graph rather than rejected.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/node/node.h
# SPA_PARAM_INFO_READ / WRITE / READWRITE / SERIAL: how a port declares each parameter.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/param/param.h
# SPA_POD_PROP_FLAG_*: per-property negotiation flags (MANDATORY, DONT_FIXATE, ...).
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/pod/pod.h
# SPA_DATA_FLAG_*: what a buffer's data block permits (READABLE, WRITABLE, MAPPABLE, ...).
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/buffer/buffer.h
# SPA_IO_CLOCK_FLAG_*, SPA_IO_VIDEO_SIZE_*, SPA_IO_SEGMENT_*: io-area flags.
# SPA_STATUS_*: spa_io_buffers.status, and what a node's process() returns.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/node/io.h
# SPA_META_HEADER_FLAG_*, SPA_META_SYNC_TIMELINE_*: buffer metadata flags.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/buffer/meta.h
# SPA_ASYNC_BIT and masks: how a queued request is encoded in a result.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/utils/result.h
# PW_PERM_*: object permissions. Octal upstream, which is exactly what a hand copy gets wrong.
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/permission.h
# SPA_TYPE_INTERFACE_*: the interface type strings an exported SPA node or device is published as.
# SPA_RESULT_TYPE_*: the result type a node or device enum_params answer carries.
# SPA_ID_INVALID: the "no id" sentinel every id-returning call uses.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/utils/defs.h
# SPA_VERSION_*: the layout version each vtable a node or loop hands out is written against.
--traverse
/usr/include/spa-0.2/spa/support/loop.h
# DRM_SYNCOBJ_*: the flags the libdrm syncobj timeline calls take (WAIT_FOR_SUBMIT, ...).
--traverse
/usr/include/libdrm/drm.h
# E*: the errno values this library returns to or matches from libpipewire, and the ones the
# hand-written libc and libdrm imports branch on. Listed one by one rather than E*: only the
# values the code uses, not the whole table.
--traverse
/usr/include/asm-generic/errno-base.h
--traverse
/usr/include/asm-generic/errno.h
# F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, SOL_SOCKET, SO_ACCEPTCONN, O_CLOEXEC: the flag and option values the hand-written
# fcntl, getsockopt and eventfd imports pass. Architecture-specific in principle, which is the reason
# to take them from the headers rather than from memory. EFD_CLOEXEC itself is an anonymous-enum
# member behind a self-referencing #define, which ClangSharp emits nothing for; the kernel defines it
# as O_CLOEXEC (linux/eventfd.h), so that is what is generated.
--traverse
/usr/include/bits/fcntl-linux.h
--traverse
/usr/include/asm-generic/socket.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/type.h
# PW_CAPABILITY_*: the keys of a stream Capability param (device-ID negotiation, the device list).
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/capabilities.h
# gnu_dev_makedev / gnu_dev_major / gnu_dev_minor: glibc exports these as real symbols behind the
# makedev/major/minor macros, and they touch no errno, so unlike the other libc calls nothing
# argues for writing them by hand. dev_t is how DMA-BUF device-ID negotiation names a device.
--traverse
/usr/include/sys/sysmacros.h

# The interface-type names and interface versions live one per object header. Traversing them is
# safe because the allowlist below only lets the matching macros through - none of the structs or
# functions these headers declare are re-emitted, so nothing collides with the ABI pass.
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/core.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/node.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/port.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/link.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/device.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/client.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/factory.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/module.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/stream.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/filter.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/proxy.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/context.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/main-loop.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/data-loop.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/global.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/impl-metadata.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/control.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/extensions/metadata.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/extensions/profiler.h
--traverse
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3/pipewire/extensions/security-context.h

# - DRM formats and modifiers -
#
# Kernel uapi, so the values are stable ABI. Generated to avoid hand-transcribing 300 fourcc codes,
# not because they drift.
--traverse
/usr/include/libdrm/drm_fourcc.h

# - libc -
#
# Only types and constants: the timespec and pollfd structs and the POLL* event bits. --traverse is
# file-granular, so naming bits/types/struct_timespec.h emits exactly that one struct rather than
# everything time.h reaches, and sys/poll.h is narrowed to pollfd by the --include list. The libc
# functions this library calls are deliberately not generated - see the note further down.
--traverse
/usr/include/bits/types/struct_timespec.h
--traverse
/usr/include/sys/poll.h
--traverse
/usr/include/bits/poll.h

# An allowlist, and the reason this pass is cheap. Without it, macro-bindings over these headers
# emits some 215 diagnostics from CompoundLiteralExpr and function-like macros it cannot translate;
# with it, only what is named here is processed and the count drops to about a dozen. It also keeps
# the traversed PipeWire headers from re-emitting structs the ABI pass already owns - only the
# matching macros come through.
#
# Globs are supported, which is what makes naming 500-odd constants practical.
--include
PW_KEY_*
--include
SPA_KEY_*
--include
PW_VERSION_*
--include
PW_TYPE_INTERFACE_*
--include
DRM_FORMAT_*
--include
SPA_NODE_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_NODE_CHANGE_MASK_*
--include
SPA_PORT_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_PORT_CHANGE_MASK_*
--include
SPA_PARAM_INFO_*
--include
SPA_POD_PROP_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_DATA_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_DEVICE_CHANGE_MASK_*
--include
SPA_DEVICE_OBJECT_CHANGE_MASK_*
--include
SPA_IO_CLOCK_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_IO_VIDEO_SIZE_*
--include
SPA_IO_SEGMENT_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_IO_SEGMENT_BAR_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_IO_SEGMENT_VIDEO_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_STATUS_*
--include
SPA_META_HEADER_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_META_SYNC_TIMELINE_*
--include
SPA_CHUNK_FLAG_*
--include
SPA_ASYNC_*
--include
PW_PERM_*
--include
PW_ID_*
--include
SPA_ID_INVALID
--include
SPA_VERSION_*
--include
DRM_SYNCOBJ_*
--include
ENOENT
--include
EINTR
--include
EIO
--include
EAGAIN
--include
ENOMEM
--include
EINVAL
--include
ENOSPC
--include
EPIPE
--include
ENOSYS
--include
ETIME
--include
EOPNOTSUPP
--include
ECONNABORTED
--include
ECONNRESET
--include
ENOTCONN
--include
ENODEV
--include
EBADF
--include
F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC
--include
SOL_SOCKET
--include
SO_ACCEPTCONN
--include
O_CLOEXEC
--include
SPA_TYPE_INTERFACE_*
--include
SPA_RESULT_TYPE_*
--include
PW_CAPABILITY_*
--include
gnu_dev_makedev
--include
gnu_dev_major
--include
gnu_dev_minor
--include
timespec
--include
pollfd
--include
POLLIN
--include
POLLERR
--include
POLLHUP
--include
POLLNVAL
--include
DRM_CAP_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE

# The libc and libdrm entry points themselves (read, write, close, poll, eventfd, fcntl,
# getsockopt, drmSyncobj*, drmGetCap) are deliberately NOT generated here, and stay hand-written as
# [LibraryImport] in Interop/NativeConstants.Libc.cs and NativeConstants.Libdrm.cs.
# This assembly sets DisableRuntimeMarshalling, and errno is load-bearing - Descriptors.cs retries
# on EINTR by reading Marshal.GetLastPInvokeError() straight after the call. ClangSharp 21.1.8
# offers only two ways to spell that and both are wrong here:
#   - SetLastError = true on a DllImport is CA1420: it requires runtime marshalling.
#   - --generate setslastsystemerror-attribute emits ClangSharp's own internal
#     [SetsLastSystemError], which is [Conditional("DEBUG")] - a documentation marker with no
#     runtime effect - and it suppresses SetLastError, so errno is never captured at all.
# It cannot emit [LibraryImport], which is the one form that both works under
# DisableRuntimeMarshalling and captures errno. Their types and constants come from this pass.

--include-directory
/usr/include/pipewire-0.3
--include-directory
/usr/include/spa-0.2
--include-directory
/usr/include/libdrm
--include-directory
/usr/include

--namespace
PipeWire.NET.Interop
--method-class-name
NativeConstants

# `timespec` is all lower-case ascii, which C# reserves (CS8981). The generated PipeWire types get
# away with their C names because they all contain an underscore.
--remap-type
timespec=PosixTimespec
--with-library-path
gnu_dev_makedev=libc
--with-library-path
gnu_dev_major=libc
--with-library-path
gnu_dev_minor=libc
--remap-type
pollfd=PosixPollFd
# gnu_dev_* take and return __dev_t, which arrives as nuint: --remap-type renames records, not
# typedefs of builtins, so it cannot say ulong. glibc's dev_t is 64 bits everywhere (__UQUAD_TYPE),
# and so is unsigned long on the 64-bit Linux targets this library ships for; DrmDevice converts.

# Internal, like the rest of the generated ABI. These are the native-facing forms - UTF-8 spans,
# libc entry points, a timespec - and none of them belong in the public surface. What a caller
# needs is the string form of the keys, and generate.sh derives that into a public class from this
# file rather than exposing this one.
--with-access-specifier
*=Internal

--generate
macro-bindings

--config
file=single
codegen=preview
types=unix
